# WordPress standards (wp-dev-kit)

Security (escape, sanitize, nonces, `$wpdb->prepare`) lives in `wp-review.md`. This file covers the rest. If a project rule is more specific (tokens, a11y, text domain, deploy), that rule wins.

## Local blueprint
- If `blueprint/02-sitemap-wireframe.md` exists, follow it for page structure when editing `theme.json`, `functions.php`, or block templates. `blueprint/` is gitignored local research. Do not commit or deploy it. Start one with `/pb:blueprint`.

## Coding standards
- Follow https://developer.wordpress.org/coding-standards/wordpress-coding-standards/. `phpcs` (config: `phpcs.xml.dist`) must report 0 errors on PHP files you touched. Pint owns formatting; WPCS owns security, i18n and WP API usage.
- When the playbook hook is installed, it runs phpcs after a PHP edit. Fix what it reports.
- Never add `phpcs:ignore` without a comment saying why.

## i18n
- Wrap every user-facing string in `__()`, `esc_html__()`, `esc_attr__()`, `_n()` or `_x()` with the project's text domain (`style.css` Text Domain, or the theme slug). A new Sage scaffold uses `sage` until the project sets its own.

## theme.json
- Tokens: follow the `sage-architecture` skill (CI and token bridging). Use the project's preset CSS variables (`var(--wp--preset--color--…)`). Never hand-edit the generated `public/build/assets/theme.json`.
- Root `theme.json`: keep `"$schema": "https://schemas.wp.org/trunk/theme.json"` and `"version": 3`. Only use keys from https://developer.wordpress.org/block-editor/reference-guides/theme-json-reference/theme-json-living/. If unsure, check; don't invent settings.

## Accessibility: WCAG 2.2 AA
Refs: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/ · https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/ · https://make.wordpress.org/themes/handbook/review/accessibility/
- Semantic HTML first: one `<h1>`, logical headings, landmarks, a working skip link.
- Everything works by keyboard alone. Focus is visible (2.4.7) and not hidden behind the sticky header (2.4.11).
- Contrast 4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for UI and large text, in each theme the project ships. Targets are at least 24×24 CSS px (2.5.8). If the project requires 44×44, use that. No drag-only actions (2.5.7).
- Visible text is part of the accessible name (2.5.3). Do not put an `aria-label` on an element that cannot take a name.
- Form fields have labels, errors are announced, `autocomplete` is set where it applies. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Build menus, tabs, modals and accordions from ARIA APG patterns, not from memory.
- Never claim "this is accessible". Say what you tested (pa11y / axe / Lighthouse) and that a manual keyboard + VoiceOver pass is still needed.

## Before a PR
- Run `wp-review`, `phpcs`, `composer analyse` (PHPStan, when it is set up), `vendor/bin/pint --test` and `npm run build`, then ask the `wp-security-reviewer` subagent to review the diff.
- One tool writes, a different tool reviews (Bugbot / Claude GitHub Action / Codex). the owner approves anything that ships.
